Subject: board: set NAND_BOOT_FLAG before probing NAND, not after

get_device_boot_flag() starts by setting the global device_boot_flag to
CARD_BOOT_FLAG and only assigns NAND_BOOT_FLAG *after* amlnf_init(0x5)
returns 0. But amlnf_init() begins with get_boot_device()
(drivers/amlnf/dev/amlnf_dev.c), which reads that very global:

	if(device_boot_flag == CARD_BOOT_FLAG){
		boot_device_flag = -1;
		aml_nand_msg("CARD BOOT: not init nand");
		return -1;
	}

so amlnf_init() bails out with -1 before amlnf_phy_init() ever runs, and
the NAND_BOOT_FLAG assignment is unreachable. Enabling CONFIG_CMD_NAND
alone therefore does not make a raw-NAND board detectable -- it only adds
a "try nand boot" line to the log followed by "CARD BOOT: not init nand".

Set the flag to NAND_BOOT_FLAG for the duration of the probe and put it
back if the probe fails, which is what the assignment was reaching for.
The eMMC branch below needs no equivalent change: emmc_init() does not
consult device_boot_flag.

Only reachable with CONFIG_CMD_NAND defined, so boards that leave it off
(the OneCloud among them) are bit-for-bit unaffected.

--- a/arch/arm/lib/board.c
+++ b/arch/arm/lib/board.c
@@ -560,10 +560,18 @@ void get_device_boot_flag(void)
 				return;
 
 			printf("try nand boot\n");
+			/*
+			 * amlnf_init() -> get_boot_device() reads the global
+			 * device_boot_flag and refuses to touch the NAND while
+			 * it still says CARD_BOOT_FLAG, so claim NAND for the
+			 * duration of the probe and hand it back on failure.
+			 */
+			device_boot_flag = NAND_BOOT_FLAG;
 			if(amlnf_init(0x5) == 0) {
 				device_boot_flag = NAND_BOOT_FLAG;
 				return;
 			}
+			device_boot_flag = CARD_BOOT_FLAG;
 			failed = true;
 		}
 #endif // CONFIG_CMD_NAND
